            <ArticleTitle>Autoimmune diseases co-existing with hepatitis C virus infection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>191-206</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">09.04/ijaai.191206</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Autoimmunity and viral infections are closely associated fields, and viruses have been proposed as a likely aetiological, contributory or triggering factors of systemic autoimmune diseases. Hepatitis C virus seems to be the virus usually associated with the appearance of autoimmune diseases, and the relationship between chronic hepatitis C virus infection and some autoimmune disease has been studied. For some of these disorders their association with hepatitis C virus infection is well recognized while for others it remains probable or weak. Examples of autoimmune phenomena observed in chronic hepatitis C virus infection include rheumatoid arthritis, thyroid disease, cryoglobulinaemia, immune thrombocytopenic purpura, systemic lupus erythematosus and sjogren syndrome. To date, the etiological role and the pathogenetic involvement of the hepatitis C infection remains unknown.The aim of this study is to assess the presence of different autoimmune manifestations of hepatitis C virus infection reported in literature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
